        "q7": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is virtual staging worth it?",
            "answer": "For most vacant or dated listings, yes. Staging can't guarantee a sale, but 97% of buyers use the internet in their home search, according to the National Association of Realtors, so the photos decide which homes get a showing. NAR's research on home staging also finds that staging makes it easier for buyers to visualize a property as their future home. For a Las Vegas buyer who may offer before flying in, staged photos are the showing — and virtually staged photos can lift online engagement with the listing — for a one-time cost per photo instead of monthly furniture rental."
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is virtual staging legal in Nevada?",
            "answer": "Yes — virtual staging is a standard marketing practice as long as it doesn't misrepresent the property, and real estate professionals should disclose it in the listing. Add furniture and decor only, don't hide defects or change permanent features, and disclose that the photos are virtually staged; the NAR Code of Ethics requires a true picture in advertising. Check Las Vegas REALTORS® MLS rules for exact labeling. Every Bella order includes disclosure guidance."
          }
        },
        "q9": {
